**Read:** [FUNdaMENTALS of Design — Alexander Slocum](../sources/FUNdaMENTALS-of-Design-Slocum.lingua#r_58Q6VQJ3FXXGXRBJ3DK0AVC3WQ), topics 1–3; [Principles and Techniques for Designing Precision Machines — Hale](../sources/Precision-Machine-Design-Hale-Thesis.lingua#r_3V7NVTW0ER7HDK3BJ1HCNPZYN4), chapter 3; [Design and Manufacturing II — MIT 2.008, 2025](../sources/Design-and-Manufacturing-II-MIT-2025.lingua#r_2FHEWYSEAHBY7X6030TW00F32E), manufacturing planning and cost.

Compare alternatives against the same service. Account for motion, force, accuracy, energy, materials, manufacturing capability, maintenance access, learning effort and recurring resources. A light assembly may require expensive process control; a simple mechanism may demand frequent replacement. Keep those consequences connected to the useful result.

A weighted score records a judgment, not an objective law. Examine how a ranking changes when weights, uncertain performance or constraints change. Remove physically infeasible candidates before giving them a favorable average score. A Pareto comparison can expose improvements and genuine tradeoffs without pretending that every criterion has the same unit.

**Work:** Compare at least three architectures, including a mechanically simpler alternative. Derive one key performance bound for each. Identify the assumptions that could reverse the choice and the smallest informative experiment. Preserve the rejected alternative and the reason it was rejected so that new evidence can reopen the decision.

